Comparing two unit values, and what each target spells it as.

A unit value is a magnitude per base, so two are equal when every base agrees.
Go gets that from struct equality and Kotlin from a data class's generated
`equals`, both field-wise without being asked. JavaScript has no such operator
for an object, and `===` is reference identity: `a == b` for two equal
measurements was false on every pair, because they are two objects. esbuild
warned about it -- "Comparison using the === operator here is always false" --
into a build log nobody read.

So the JS is now the conjunction over the bases, which is what the other two
targets were already doing. `!=` is its negation rather than a second walk, so
the two cannot drift apart.

`single` is the control and the thing that must not regress: a single-base unit
is a plain number on every target, where `===` is already correct and a
per-base walk would be five comparisons to answer what one answers. It has to
keep compiling to the bare operator, which is what the deny below states.

`cmd/sngl/testdata/unit_equality_runs.txt` runs this same shape under
bubbletea, the interpreter and a real Chromium, because a golden shows the
emitted text and only executing it shows the answer.

deny out/none/html/index.html `a__inst0 === state\.b__inst0` -- reference identity on two records is false for every equal pair; the comparison has to be per base
deny out/none/html/index.html `wide__inst0 !== state\.` -- the same for !=, which is the negation of the per-base conjunction
deny out/none/html/index.html `state\.d__inst0\.ms` -- a single-base unit is a plain number, not a record with an ms key to walk

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:time"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main ui.node {
    var a ui.measurement = 3px
    var b ui.measurement = 3px
    var wide ui.measurement = 3px + 2em
    var d time.duration = 500ms
    var e time.duration = 1s

    var same = a == b
    var apart = wide != a
    var single = d == e

    ui.text(value="{same} {apart} {single}")
}

ui.window {
    main
}
